# Easiest: run  npm run setup  — it creates .env from this file with fresh random secrets filled in.
# Or copy this file to .env and replace the placeholders yourself before running `docker compose up`.
#
# Email, malware scanning and Google Drive do NOT have to be set in this file: the installation's first
# administrator can enter them in the web app (Server settings) with no restart. Anything you DO set here wins
# over the web app, which then shows it as locked. The values below that are NOT optional (database, secrets, addresses)
# can only live in this file.

# Postgres superuser password (used only by the one-shot migrate step to create the app's own,
# much more restricted, database role).
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me

# Password for the app's own low-privilege database role (portal_app). Different from the one above.
APP_DB_PASSWORD=change-me-too

# Signs session tokens for the built-in email+password login. Generate a real one with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
JWT_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-value

# Where the app itself is reachable from a browser — used to build links in invite emails.
APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5173

# Where the frontend should send API requests. Baked into the frontend build, so changing this
# requires rebuilding the frontend image (docker compose build frontend).
API_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Only if your network blocks registry.npmjs.org (the Docker build then fails with ECONNRESET or ERR_SSL_WRONG_VERSION_NUMBER):
# download the libraries from a mirror instead. Every download is still checked against package-lock.json.
# NPM_REGISTRY=https://registry.yarnpkg.com

# --- Without Docker (node scripts/local.mjs) -----------------------------------------------------------
# Only if your PostgreSQL is NOT on this computer's port 5432 with the administrator "postgres". The password above
# (POSTGRES_PASSWORD) must then be the one PostgreSQL itself was installed with (on Windows: chosen in its installer).
# POSTGRES_PORT=5432
# POSTGRES_USER=postgres
# POSTGRES_HOST=127.0.0.1
# --- Google Drive (optional until an org admin clicks "Connect Google Drive") ---------------------
# Prefer the web app: Server settings -> Google Drive app. Only use these lines if you want it fixed in this file.
# Create an OAuth client at https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials ("Web application"),
# enable the Google Drive API, and add GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI below as an authorised redirect URI.
# Set all three or none of them — a half-configured client stops the server at startup on purpose.
# The app only asks for the narrow `drive.file` scope: it can see files and folders it created itself,
# never the rest of your Drive.
# They're commented out so a fresh copy of this file starts cleanly with Drive switched off. Remove the
# leading "# " from all four lines below once you have real values.
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Must be reachable from the admin's browser and end in /oauth/google/callback
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/oauth/google/callback

# Encrypts secrets stored in the database (32 bytes, hex): the Google refresh tokens, the authenticator-app
# secrets for two-step sign-in, and the mail password and Google client secret saved in the web app. Required for
# any of those to work. `npm run setup` fills this in for you; or generate with:  openssl rand -hex 32
# Treat it like a database password and back it up. If you lose or change it, every org has to reconnect Drive
# AND every authenticator code stops working (people then sign in with a recovery code, or an org admin resets them).
# SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# --- Browser access to the API ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated list of browser origins allowed to call the API. Leave this line out entirely and it
# defaults to just APP_BASE_URL's origin, which is right when the frontend is served from there.
# Set it to an empty value to switch cross-origin access off (e.g. behind a reverse proxy that serves
# frontend and API from one origin). Entries must be full origins, e.g. https://portal.example
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://portal.example,https://staging.portal.example

# --- Behind a reverse proxy? -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The sign-in, invite and password-reset endpoints limit how often one address can call them. If a reverse
# proxy (nginx, Caddy, a cloud load balancer...) sits in front of the API, every request seems to come from
# that proxy unless you say how many proxies there are. Leave at 0 when browsers connect to the API
# directly (the default docker compose setup); set 1 for a single proxy in front. A count, never "true":
# trusting every forwarded address would let anyone choose which address they appear to come from.
# TRUST_PROXY=1

# --- Where evidence files are kept --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Each organisation picks its storage in the web app (Integrations): Google Drive, a folder on this server's disk,
# or any S3-compatible service (Amazon S3, MinIO, Ceph, Wasabi, Backblaze B2, Cloudflare R2...). Two lines here
# decide what is on offer for the disk and the S3 options.
#
# A folder on THIS server's disk. Leave unset and the "this server's disk" option is not offered. Must be an
# absolute path that already exists and that the portal's user can write to. Back this folder up: it IS the evidence.
# Windows:  LOCAL_STORAGE_DIR=D:/EvidenceFiles       Linux:  LOCAL_STORAGE_DIR=/var/lib/evidence-portal/files
# Docker:   LOCAL_STORAGE_DIR=/data/evidence  (the compose file keeps that folder in its own volume, evidence-files)
# LOCAL_STORAGE_DIR=
#
# S3 services on a private network (your own MinIO or Ceph at 10.x.x.x / 192.168.x.x / localhost) are refused
# by default, so a shared installation cannot be pointed at things inside its own network. Set to true ONLY when this
# installation is your organisation's own. Cloud metadata addresses (169.254.x.x) are refused whatever this says.
# STORAGE_ALLOW_PRIVATE_ADDRESSES=false

# --- Email (invitations and evidence reminders) ------------------------------------------------------
# Any SMTP server works: Microsoft 365, Google Workspace, Amazon SES, Postmark, SendGrid, your own Postfix...
# Leave SMTP_HOST unset and the portal still runs, but emails are NOT sent: they are printed to the server
# log instead. Invite links work like passwords, so don't run a real deployment that way.
# Prefer the web app: Server settings -> Email. Only use these lines if you want it fixed in this file.
# Remove the leading "# " from the lines you need.
#
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# SMTP_SECURITY=starttls        # starttls (port 587, the default) | tls (port 465) | none (local mail catcher only)
# SMTP_PORT=587                 # optional: defaults to the usual port for SMTP_SECURITY
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=                # set both or neither; refused together with SMTP_SECURITY=none
# EMAIL_FROM="Evidence Portal <noreply@yourfirm.example>"

# --- Malware scanning (ClamAV) ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every uploaded file is streamed to a ClamAV daemon (clamd) before it is recorded. Leave CLAMD_HOST unset
# and uploads are recorded as "skipped" (never "clean"), so reviewers can see nothing checked them.
# If ClamAV is configured but unreachable, uploads are refused with a retryable error rather than
# recorded unchecked. Prefer the web app: Server settings -> Malware scanning. In this file instead: with Docker
# Compose, set CLAMD_HOST=clamav and start with  --profile scanning
# (ClamAV needs roughly 2 GB of memory and downloads its signatures on first start, which takes a few minutes).
#
# CLAMD_HOST=clamav
# CLAMD_PORT=3310
# CLAMD_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
# CLAMD_MAX_FILE_MB=25          # keep at or below clamd's StreamMaxLength (25 MB by default); bigger files are marked "unscannable"
